{"subjectId":"c","id":"typedef","title":"typedef","group":"Structures & User-Defined Types","order":38,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>typedef</code> creates an <em>alias</em> for an existing type — shorter names, clearer intent and a single place to change a type. It never creates a new, distinct type.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;\n\ntypedef unsigned long ulong;                 /* shorter name */\ntypedef int32_t Meters;                      /* documents intent (still an int32_t) */\ntypedef struct { double re, im; } Complex;   /* anonymous struct, used without 'struct' */\ntypedef int (*Compare)(int, int);            /* function pointer type */\ntypedef char Name[32];                       /* array type */\n\nint ascending(int a, int b) { return a - b; }\n\nint main(void) {\n    ulong big = 123456789UL;\n    Meters height = 8849;\n    Complex z = {1.5, -2.0};\n    Compare cmp = ascending;\n    Name who = \"Tenzing\";\n    printf(\"%lu %d %.1f%+.1fi %d %s %zu\\n\", big, height, z.re, z.im, cmp(3, 1), who, sizeof(Name));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>123456789 8849 1.5-2.0i 2 Tenzing 32</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-packages\"></i>typedef struct patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;\n\n/* tag + typedef: the tag is needed for the self-reference inside the body */\ntypedef struct Node {\n    int value;\n    struct Node *next;\n} Node;\n\nint main(void) {\n    Node second = {2, NULL};\n    Node first = {1, &amp;second};\n    struct Node *same = &amp;first;          /* both names work */\n    printf(\"%d %d\\n\", same-&gt;value, first.next-&gt;value);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>typedef vs #define</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n#define PTR_D int *\ntypedef int *PtrT;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 1, y = 2;\n    PTR_D a, b;       /* expands to: int *a, b;  -&gt; b is an int! */\n    PtrT  c, d;       /* both are int *                         */\n    a = &amp;x; b = y;\n    c = &amp;x; d = &amp;y;\n    printf(\"%zu %zu %zu %zu\\n\", sizeof a, sizeof b, sizeof c, sizeof d);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>8 4 8 8</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use <code>typedef</code> for function-pointer types, opaque handles and platform-dependent types (<code>size_t</code>, <code>uint32_t</code> are typedefs).</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Hide pointers behind typedefs for ordinary data (<code>typedef struct node *NodePtr;</code>) — readers lose track of what is a pointer. The Linux kernel style bans most struct typedefs for this reason.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Names ending in <code>_t</code> are reserved by POSIX for future system types; in portable code prefer <code>Name</code> or <code>name_type</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Does typedef create a new type?</summary><div><p>No. It creates an alias: <code>typedef int Meters;</code> makes <code>Meters</code> and <code>int</code> fully interchangeable, with no extra type checking. To get a distinct type in C, wrap the value in a single-member <code>struct</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}